module literal_arm.neg_1

// A `match` ARM ON A STRING LITERAL -- READ, JUDGED, AND REFUSED BY NAME.
//
// THIS CELL WAS POSITIVE UNTIL 2026-09-20 08:11 AND IT ASSERTED SOMETHING
// FALSE. The parser learnt string and char arm patterns that night and the
// fixture recorded the win; nobody ran the differential, and the differential
// is what said the rest: novac DIED on this file with `emit_c: match pattern
// is neither an int literal nor an identifier`. The form reached an emitter
// that had no arm for it.
//
// So the cell changes sides rather than disappearing, and what it guards is
// the ORDER OF PREFERENCE, not the wording: a named refusal is louder than a
// crash and quieter than a wrong answer. The day the lowering can compare a
// string, this becomes a positive again -- and the cell will have held the
// line in between instead of leaving a hole where a green light used to be.
//
// What the parser wave was really worth is unchanged and measured: `diag`
// fell from 22 diagnostics to 2 and `lex` from 76 to 31 on these two forms.
// Reading them was the win; compiling them is the next one.

// NOVAC_EXPECT a `match` arm on a string or char literal is read but not compiled yet

fn word_kind(s str) -> int => match s {
    // ONE string arm, because a negative cell seeds ONE cause: the door
    // refuses each arm it meets, so a second string arm here would be a
    // second diagnostic about the same missing capability.
    "match" => 2
    _ => 0
}
